what we need to see is inflation coming  down  in a clear and convincing way i think  that bringing inflation down should be  our number one priority the story of  inflation is changing  a year ago wall street a lot of policy  makers in washington were hoping that  the inflation uptick would be what they  call transitory there was a decent  amount of evidence to support that view  that it would be temporary or transitory  that it would come and go away well  that's not what happened to understand  where inflation is spreading in the  average american's household budget we  looked to the labor department's  inflation metric the consumer price  index we came away with some surprises  and some lessons to tell us how the  story of inflation is changing the  inflation we're currently experiencing  began as pandemic lockdown restrictions  were lifted triggering a boom in demand  when inflation turned up in 2021 it was  a different kind of inflation than what  we're seeing right now there were really  two facets to it one was we saw these  idiosyncratic changes for instance used  car prices jumped up in part because  there were shortages of new cars supply  chain bottlenecks and chip shortages  pushed up prices for goods like cars  both new and old and then the other  thing that we saw was prices for other  goods uh jumping up during the pandemic  people  binged on goods they couldn't go out to  restaurants they couldn't go to hotels  and so they ordered amazon and  the spending picked up on a lot of goods  and then as the pandemic receded a  little bit we we had these supply  shortages for imported goods in  particular so you saw things like  furniture prices go up apparel prices  shoe prices stuff that used to be really  cheap because we were importing it from  low-cost labor places was all of a  sudden popping up  but the inflation we've seen recently in  2022 is more severe and more  wide-reaching now we're seeing prices  for services like one of my favorite  indicators is haircuts for 20 years the  price of a haircut was going up between  zero and four percent in this latest  stage they've gone up uh five percent  because the cost of labor is going up  some of the most noticeable recent price  hikes have been at the grocery store  thirteen percent of the average  americans household budget goes to food  and since last april household food  prices are up almost eleven 11  grocery prices were pretty tame actually  last year and what we've seen this year  is increases at double digit levels  since last year prices for ground beef  have risen almost 15 percent and prices  for chicken breasts are up 16.4  so meat is a very interesting case when  you're building a herd  uh this year you're building it for two  or three years out you have to have some  view of what the world is going to look  like in a couple of years cows don't  grow overnight right in 2020  when some of these herds were being  built we were going into a devastating  recession now two years later we're  dealing with with meat shortages because  there's this under supply of meat  meanwhile a combination of factors  caused gas prices to soar  the russia's invasion of ukraine earlier  this year jolted the global market  prompting oil price increases russia's  invasion of ukraine has taken things  like gasoline prices from a stage where  they were recovering from the pandemic  to where they're much higher than they  were before in late may gasoline prices  topped four dollars a gallon in all 50  states for the first time ever currently  california leads the country with prices  over six dollars the war on ukraine has  had a big impact on on global energy  markets when coveted hit  we hit went into this global downturn  the price of gasoline in the u.s went  from around three dollars a gallon to  two dollars a gallon in 2021 but by  early 22 it was back up to over three  dollars a gallon so there was a recovery  we had been through a full cycle then  russia invaded ukraine  and now we have gasoline prices over  five dollars a gallon in many places  higher energy prices have also bled into  air travel analysts say that rising fuel  costs and staffing shortages have led to  a spike in airfares in april airline  fares surged 18.6 from a month earlier  the fastest rise on record americans are  really frustrated right now with what's  happening and one great place to see  that is in what's happening at the  airport flights are being canceled at  the same time that airfares are going up  we also found that the biggest budget  item for most households the cost of  keeping a roof over your head is heading  up housing makes up 30 of the average  household's budget and it's up 5 from a  year ago so rents are another facet of  this inflation problem it relates to  under supply of housing that was built  really over the last 10 years since the  2008 financial crisis and led to less  building  for over a decade and now we're paying a  price for that because we're seeing  price increases  for homes  and that's feeding through into rental  prices we now are entering a period of  transition  from one of historic recovery to one  that can be marked by stable and steady  growth  making this shift is a central piece of  the president's plan to get inflation  under control  without sacrificing the economic gains  we've made as the country heads into the  midterm election season much of the  political discussion is centered around  the damaging impact of inflation a  meeting with chairman today and  secretary yellen  to discuss my top priority and that is  addressing inflation the fed has shifted  its strategy over the last few months  what they're trying to do now and what  they think they have to do is hit the  demand side of the economy and they're  going to do that by raising interest  rates to make it more expensive to  borrow the fed is trying to hit demand  to slow growth economic growth just  enough  so that inflation comes down to more  manageable levels of around two percent  a year so one risk is that they don't  hit demand enough  and inflation remains elevated the other  thing that could go wrong is that they  hit demand so hard that not only do they  bring down inflation  but they drive the whole economy into  recession and in that scenario the real  risk that people need to worry about is  that in the process of going into a  recession they kill this great job  market and unemployment starts rising  [Music]
 

