  and now we have gasoline prices over  five dollars a gallon in many places  higher energy prices have also bled into  air travel analysts say that rising fuel  costs and staffing shortages have led to  a spike in airfares in april airline  fares surged 18.6 from a month earlier  the fastest rise on record americans are  really frustrated right now with what's  happening and one great place to see  that is in what's happening at the  airport flights are being canceled at  the same time that airfares are going up  we also found that the biggest budget  item for most households the cost of  keeping a roof over your head is heading  up housing makes up 30 of the average  household's budget and it's up 5 from a  year ago so rents are another facet of  this inflation problem it relates to  under supply of housing that was built  really over the last 10 years since the  2008 financial crisis and led to less  building  for over a decade and now we're paying a  price for that because we're seeing  price increases  for homes  and that's feeding through into rental  prices we now are entering a period of  transition  from one of historic recovery to one  that can be marked by stable and steady  growth  making this shift is a central piece of  the president's plan to get inflation  under control  without sacrificing the economic gains  we've made as the country heads into the  midterm election season much of the  political discussion is centered around  the damaging impact of inflation a  meeting with chairman today and  secretary yellen  to discuss my top priority and that is  addressing inflation the fed has shifted  its strategy over the last few months  what they're trying to do now and what  they think they have to do is hit the  demand side of the economy and they're  going to do that by raising interest  rates to make it more expensive to  borrow the fed is trying to hit demand  to slow growth economic growth just  enough  so that inflation comes down to more  manageable levels of around two percent  a year so one risk is that they don't  hit demand enough  and inflation remains elevated the other  thing that could go wrong is that they  hit demand so hard that not only do they  bring down inflation  but they drive the whole economy into  recession and in that scenario the real  risk that people need to worry about is  that in the process of going into a  recession they kill this great job  market and unemployment starts rising  [Music]


